                <pb facs="0612" n="500" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg500"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">794.00/91</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1607</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-12-27">December 27, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received January 13,
                        1936.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to enclose herewith a
                    memorandum of a conversation of Mr. Dickover, a member of the staff of this
                    Embassy, with Mr. Saburo Kurusu, the Chief of the Bureau of Commercial Affairs
                    of the Japanese Foreign Office, on December 22, 1935. Mr. Kurusu spoke frankly,
                    and undoubtedly reflected what is in the minds of many Japanese today. The
                    conversation is interesting, in that it is seldom that a Japanese, especially
                    one of high official position, will allow himself to express the aims of his
                    people so freely.</p>
                <p>While Mr. Kurusu spoke so frankly principally through a feeling of friendliness,
                    he probably had in mind also the idea of conveying to his listener the thought
                    that it would be best to leave Japan alone to work out her destiny in the Far
                    East.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph C. Grew</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d472-a1.xml" xpointer="d472-a1"/>
